paper/src/fallbcol.tex:146:\end{theorem}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:148:\begin{theorem}\label{theo:mainfall}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:149:Let $G,H$ be graphs, and $\odot$ denote a graph product. If ${\cal F}(G)\neq \emptyset$ and ${\cal F}(H)\neq \emptyset$, then ${\cal F}(G\odot H)\neq \emptyset$. Also,
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:151:\item If $\odot$ is either the lexicographic product, or the strong product, or the co-normal product, then
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:153:\item If $\odot$ is the cartesian product, then
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:155:\item And if $\odot$ is the direct product, then% or the modular product, then
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:158:\end{theorem}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:160:We mention that our results also give information about the b-spectrum and fall-spectrum of the products.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:162:%In addition to these, we investigate the classical chromatic number of the direct product of graphs. This is motivated by the following famous long standing conjecture.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:170:Our article is organized as follows. In Section~\ref{sec:hom}, we present the main definitions and the results concerning b-homomorphisms and Type II homomorphisms of products of graphs. In Section~\ref{sec:products}, we present the formal definition of the main graph products, analyse the structure of the products of complete graphs, and present bounds for the metrics on these products. The results on these two sections produce Theorems~\ref{theo:mainb} and~\ref{theo:mainfall}. In Section~\ref{sec:fallPart} we present some cases where ${\cal F}(G\odot H)$ can be non-empty even though ${\cal F}(H)$ is empty. %, and in Section~\ref{sec:hed}, we present our partial results on the Hedetniemi's Conjecture. 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:171: Finally, in Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} we present some questions left open, and show an example that give a negative answer to a question posed by Kaul and Mitillos about fall-colorings of perfect graphs~\cite{KM.16}.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:176:\section{Homomorphisms and products}\label{sec:hom}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:178:Given graphs $G$ and $H$, a \emph{graph product} $\odot$ on $G$ and $H$ is a graph $F$ such that $V(F)=V(G)\times V(H)$, and $\alpha\beta\in E(F)$ if and only if some condition $P_\odot(G,H,\alpha,\beta)$ is satisfied.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:181:    We say that $\odot$ is an \emph{adjacency product} if $P_\odot(G,H,\alpha,\beta)$ is a composition of a subset of the following formulas, where $\alpha=(u_a,v_a)$ and $\beta=(u_b,v_b)$: 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:184:These are called \emph{basic formulas related to $(\alpha,\beta)$}, where $\alpha,\beta\in V(G)\times V(H)$. In Section~\ref{sec:products}, we present the formal definitions of the main studied adjacency products. The next proposition will be very useful throughout this section.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:186:\begin{proposition}\label{prop:basic}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:187:Let $G,H,G',H'$ be graphs, $\odot$ be an adjacency product, and consider vertices $\alpha,\beta \in V(G\odot H)$, and $\alpha'\beta'\in V(G'\odot H')$. If for every basic formula $\gamma(G,H,\alpha,\beta)$ in ${\cal B}(G,H,\alpha,\beta)$ we have that $\gamma(G,H,\alpha,\beta)$ implies $\gamma(G',H',\alpha',\beta')$, then
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:189:\end{proposition}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:191:The next lemma tell us that graph homomorphisms are well behaved under adjacency products.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:193:\begin{lemma}\label{lem:hom}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:194:Let $G$, $H$ and $F$ be graphs and $\odot$ be an adjacency product. If $H\Hom F$, then $(G\odot H)\Hom (G\odot F)$ and $(H\odot G)\Hom (F\odot G)$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:195:\end{lemma}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:197:Let $f$ be a homomorphism from $H$ to $F$, and denote $(G\odot H)$ and $(G\odot F)$ by $H',F'$, respectively. We prove that $H'\Hom F'$, and the other part of the lemma is analogous. For this, let $g:V(H')\Hom V(F')$ be defined as $g((u,v)) = (u,f(v))$. Let $\alpha\beta\in E(H')$; we need to prove that $g(\alpha)g(\beta)\in E(F')$. 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:202:Clearly $v_a=v_b$ implies $f(v_a)=f(v_b)$, and since $f$ is a homomorphism we know that $v_av_b\in E(H)$ implies $f(v_a)f(v_b)\in E(F)$. The lemma follows by Proposition~\ref{prop:basic} and the fact that $\alpha\beta\in E(G\odot H)$, i.e., $P_\odot(G,H,\alpha,\beta)$ holds. 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:206:%As we will see later in the text, the products of complete graphs tell us important informations about the existence of the investigated colorings of products of graphs. Thus, we start by analysing the structure of these products. 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:219:\begin{proposition}[\cite{SLS.16}]
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:221:\end{proposition}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:223:The following lemma is analogous to Lemma~\ref{lem:hom} and have been proved in~\cite{SLS.16} for the lexicographic product. 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:225:\begin{lemma}\label{lem:bhom}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:226:Let $G$, $H$, and $F$ be graphs and $\odot$ be an adjacency product. If $H\bhom F$, then $(G\odot H)\bhom (G\odot F)$ and $(H\odot G)\bhom (F\odot G)$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:227:\end{lemma}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:229:Let $f$ be a b-homomorphism from $H$ to $F$, and denote $G\odot H$ and $G\odot F$ by $H',F'$, respectively. Define $g:V(H')\rightarrow V(F')$ as $g((u,v)) = (u,f(v))$. We prove that $g$ is a b-homomorphism; the other part of the theorem is analogous.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:231:  By Lemma~\ref{lem:hom}, we know that $g$ is a homomorphism. So now consider $\alpha=(u_a,v_a)\in V(F')$; we need to show that there exists $\alpha'=(u'_a,v'_a)\in g^{-1}(\alpha)$ such that $g(N_{H'}(\alpha')) = N_{F'}(\alpha)$. Because $f$ is a b-homomorphism, there exists $v'_a\in f^{-1}(v_a)$ such that $f(N_H(v'_a)) = N_{\cal F}(v_a)$. So let $\alpha' = (u_a,v'_a)$, and consider any $\beta = (u_b,v_b)\in N_{F'}(\alpha)$. 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:238:We want to find $v'_b$ such that $g(\beta') = \beta$ and that makes the basic formulas in the first equation imply the basic formulas in the second. If this is the case, then Proposition~\ref{prop:basic} implies $\beta'\in N_{F'}(\alpha')$ and we are done. 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:244:\begin{corollary}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:245:Let $G,H,G',H'$ be graphs and $\odot$ be an adjacency product. If $G\bhom G'$ and $H\bhom H'$, then $(G\odot H)\bhom (G'\odot H')$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:246:\end{corollary}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:248:\begin{corollary}\label{cor:bspectrum}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:249:Let $G,H$ be graphs and $\odot$ be an adjacency product. Then:
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:253:\end{corollary}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:255:It is known that, contrary to the chromatic number, the b-chromatic number is not a monotonic parameter, i.e., a graph $G$ might have a subgraph $H$ such that $b(H) > b(G)$. For instance, let $H$ be obtained from the complete bipartite graph $K_{3,3}$ by removing a perfect matching, and let $G$ be obtained from $H$ by adding vertices $u,v$, edge $uv$ and making $u$ complete to one of the parts of $H$ and $v$ to the other. One can verify that $b(G) = 2 < b(H) = 3$. This is why we cannot ensure that the maximum value in the sets of Corollary \ref{cor:bspectrum} are attained when $k=b(G)$ and $k'=b(H)$. Nevertheless, we get:
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:257:\begin{corollary}\label{lem:bGdotH}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:261:\end{corollary}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:263:%Note that for any two positive integers $p,q$, if $p\le q$, then $K_p\odot F$ is an induced subgraph of $K_q\odot F$, for every graph $F$, the same being valid for $F\odot K_p$ and $F\odot K_q$. The lemma follows by Corollary~\ref{cor:bspectrum} and Proposition~\ref{prop:induced}.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:275:Given graphs $G$ and $H$, a function $f:V(G)\rightarrow V(H)$ is a \emph{domatic homomorphism} if for every $u'\in V(H)$, $v'\in N(u')$, and $u\in f^{-1}(u')$, there exists $v\in f^{-1}(v)$ such that $uv\in E(G)$. Observe that a domatic homomorphism is not necessarily a homomorphism. In~\cite{LL.09}, the authors define a \emph{Type II homomorphism} as being a homomorphism which is also a domatic homomorphism. They then prove that the existence of such an homomorphism is a transitive relation, and investigate the cartesian product of graphs, in particular of two trees. If there exists a domatic homomorphism or Type II homomorphism from $G$ to $H$, then we write $G\Dom H$ or $G\TII H$, respectivelly. 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:276:We want to prove an analogous version of Lemma~\ref{lem:bhom} for Type II homomorphisms. Because of Lemma~\ref{lem:hom}, we only need to prove the following.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:278:%From now on, given an adjacency product $\odot$, the part of $P_\odot$ depending on $u_a,u_b$ is denoted by $P^1_\odot$, while the part depending on $v_a,v_b$ is denoted by $P^2_\odot$. For instance, if $P_\odot((u_a,v_a),(u_b,v_b)) = (u_a=u_b) \vee (v_a=v_b \vee v_av_b\in E(H))$, then $P^1_\odot((u_a,v_a),(u_b,v_b)) = (u_a=u_b)$, while $P^2_\odot = (v_a=v_b \vee v_av_b\in E(H))$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:281:\begin{lemma}\label{lem:dom}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:282:Let $G$, $H$, and $F$ be graphs and $\odot$ be an adjacency product. If there exists a surjective domatic homomorphism $f$ from $H$ to $F$, then $(G\odot H)\Dom (G\odot F)$ and $(H\odot G)\Dom (F\odot G)$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:283:\end{lemma}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:302:Observe that if $H$ does not have any isolated vertices, then every domatic homomorphism into $H$ is also surjective. Therefore, we get the following corollary.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:304:\begin{corollary}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:305:Let $G,H,G',H'$ be graphs and $\odot$ be an adjacency product. If $G'$ and $H'$ have no isolated vertices, $G\TII G'$ and $H\TII H'$, then $(G\odot H)\TII (G'\odot H')$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:306:\end{corollary}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:310:\begin{lemma}[\cite{LL.09}]
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:312:\end{lemma}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:314:Also, note that if $k\in {\cal F}(G)$, then there exists a surjective Type II homomorphism from $G$ to $K_k$. As a corollary, we get:
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:316:\begin{corollary}\label{cor:FallSpectrum}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:317:Let $G,H$ be graphs and $\odot$ be an adjacency product. Then:
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:323:\end{corollary}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:327:\begin{lemma}\label{lem:fallPar}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:328:Let $G,H$ be graphs and $\odot$ be an adjacency product, and let ${\cal F}$ denote the set $\bigcup_{p\in {\cal F}(G),q\in {\cal F}(H)}{\cal F}(K_p\odot K_q)$. If ${\cal F}(G)\neq$ and ${\cal F}(H)\neq\emptyset$, then $\emptyset\neq {\cal F} \subseteq {\cal F}(G\odot H)$, and:
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:332:\end{lemma}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:334:%Observe that the corollary above also gives us that if ${\cal F}(H)\neq \emptyset$, then ${\cal F}(G\odot H)\neq \emptyset$, ${\cal F}(H\odot G)\neq \emptyset$, and: FALSO, EH NECESSARIO QUE ${\cal F}(G\odot K_k)\neq \emptyset$ ou ${\cal F}(K_k\odot H)$ para algum $k\in {\cal F}(H)$ ou $k\in {\cal F}(G)$
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:345:\section{Adjacency products of complete graphs}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:346:\label{sec:products}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:348:In this section we investigate the parameters of $K_p\odot K_q$ for the main adjacency products. The table below defines the condition $P_\odot$ for each of these products. If $uv\in E(G)$, we write $u\sim v$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:381:\label{tab:products}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:385:First, we investigate the structure of the product $K_p\odot K_q$. We write $G\cong H$ if $G$ and $H$ are isomorphic graphs.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:387:\begin{proposition}\label{prop:Lex}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:388:Let $p,q$ be positive integers and $\odot$ be either the lexicographic product, the strong product, or the co-normal product. Then \[K_p\odot K_q  \cong K_{pq}.\]
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:389:\end{proposition}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:397:Now, suppose $j=k$, in which case $i\neq h$. We get the same situation as before for the lexicographic product and for the co-normal product. Also, $\alpha\beta\in E(K_p\boxtimes K_q)$, since $u_i\sim u_h$ and $v_j=v_k$. Finally, suppose that $i=h$ and $j\neq k$. Then, $\alpha\beta\in E(K_p[K_q])\cap E(K_p\boxtimes K_q)$, since $u_i=u_h$ and $v_j\sim v_k$, while $\alpha\beta\in E(K_p*K_q)$ since $v_j\sim v_k$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:401:By the proposition above, we get that the value $pq$ is in the b-spectrum of $G\odot H$ for every $p\in S_b(G)$ and $q\in S_b(H)$, the same being valid for the fall-spectrum. This and Lemmas~\ref{lem:bGdotH} and~\ref{lem:fallPar} give us the corollary below and part of Theorems~\ref{theo:mainb} and~\ref{theo:mainfall}. We mention that $b(G\odot H)\ge b(G)b(H)$ has been proved in~\cite{JP.15}, when $\odot$ is either the lexicographic product or the strong product. Our result generalizes theirs, and we mention that, if more is learned about $b(G\odot K_p)$,  then Corollary~\ref{cor:bspectrum} can actually produce better bounds than the ones given in Theorems~\ref{theo:mainb} and~\ref{theo:mainfall}. 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:403:\begin{corollary}\label{cor:Lex}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:404:Let $G,H$ be graphs, and $\odot$ be the lexicographic, strong or co-normal product. Also, let $T$ denote either $S_b$ or ${\cal F}$. Then,
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:408:\end{corollary}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:410:%Next, we make an analogous study for the cartesian product and the homomorphic product.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:413:%\begin{proposition}\label{prop:HomCart}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:415:%\end{proposition}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:420:Now, we analyse the colorings of the cartesian products. The following proposition will be useful.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:422:\begin{proposition}\label{prop:Cart}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:424:\end{proposition}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:426:Consider $p\le q$, denote $K_p\oblong K_q$ by $G$, and write $V(K_p)$ as $\{u_1,\cdots,u_p\}$ and $V(K_q)$ as $\{v_1,\cdots,v_q\}$. First, we show that ${\cal F}(G)\neq \emptyset$. For this, let $f:V(G)\rightarrow \{1,\cdots,q\}$ be defined as follows: for every $j\in \{1,\cdots,q\}$, set $f((u_1,v_j))$ to $ j$; then color each subsequent $V(u_i)$ with a distinct chaotic permutation of $(1,\cdots,q)$ (there are enough permutations since $q\ge p$). Because every vertex is within a clique of size $q$, we get that $f$ is a fall-coloring. It remains to prove that no other fall-coloring exists. So, suppose by contradiction that $m\in {\cal F}(K_p\oblong K_q)\setminus\{q\}$, and let $f$ be a fall-coloring of $G$ with $m$ colors. Because $m>q$, there must exist a color $d$ that does not appear in $f(V(u_1))$. But since every vertex in $V(u_1)$ is a b-vertex and $V(u_i)$ is a clique for every $i\in \{1,\cdots,p\}$, this means that color $d$ must appear in $(u_{i_1},v_1),\cdots,(u_{i_q},v_q)$ for distinct values of $i_1,\cdots,i_q$, none of which can be~1. We get a contradiction since in this case we have $p\ge q+1$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:428:%This and a result presented in~\cite{JO.12} allow us to get the following theorem.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:429:We mention that the existence of a fall-coloring of $K_p\oblong K_q$ with $q$ colors has been first observed in~\cite{LL.09}, and that it already implies the part concerning the fall-spectrum in the lemma below. Nevertheless, Proposition~\ref{prop:Cart} tell us that, in order to get  bounds better than the one given by Theorem~\ref{theo:mainfall}, one needs to investigate ${\cal F}(G\oblong K_p)$ when $G$ is not the complete graph.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:431:\begin{corollary}\label{cor:Cart}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:438:\end{corollary}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:440:First, let $k\in S_b(G)$ be such that $k\ge \chi(H)$. By Proposition~\ref{prop:Cart}, we get $k\in S_b(K_k\oblong K_{\chi(H)})$. %By Proposition~\ref{prop:HomCart}, we get that $k\in S_b(K_k\ltimes K_{\chi(H)})$, and b
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:441:By Corollary~\ref{cor:bspectrum} and the fact that $\chi(H)\in S_b(H)$, we get that $k\in S_b(G\oblong H)$. Similarly, if $k\in S_b(H)$ is such that $k\ge \chi(G)$, we get that $k\in S_b(K_{\chi(G)}\oblong K_k)\subseteq S_b(G\oblong H)$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:443:%Now, suppose that $\chi(G)\le b(H)\le b(G)$, and let $p\in S_b(H)$. By a result presented in~\cite{JO.12}, we know that there exists a b-coloring $f$ of $K_p\oblong K_p$ with $2p-3$ colors. We extend $f$ to $K_p\oblong K_q$, where $q=b(G)$, which implies that $p\in S_b(G\oblong H)$ because of Corollary~\ref{cor:bspectrum}. For this, denote a vertex of $K_p\oblong K_q$ by $(i,j)$ and let $f'$ be obtained from $f$ as follows: for each $i\in \{p+1,\cdots, q\}$
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:444:Finally, let $k\in {\cal F}(G)$ and $k'\in {\cal F}(H)$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:FallSpectrum} and Proposition~\ref{prop:Cart}, we know that $\max\{k,k'\}\in {\cal F}(G\oblong H)$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:448:In~\cite{KM.02}, the authors prove that $b(G\oblong H)\ge \max\{b(G),b(H)\}$. Observe that this also follows from the corollary above.%The corollary below generalizes their result.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:450:%\begin{theorem}\label{theo:Cartb}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:451:%Let $G,H$ be graphs, and suppose that $b(G)\ge b(H)$. Then, %and $\odot$ be the cartesian product or the homomorphic product. Then,
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:453:%\end{theorem}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:455:%By Corollary~\ref{theo:Cart}, we know that $b(G\oblong H)\ge \max\{b(G),b(H)\} = b(G)$. So we just need to prove that $b(G\oblong H)\ge 2b(H)-3$. This is trivial if $b(G)\ge 2b(H)-3$, so suppose otherwise and let $p=b(H)$ and $q=b(G)$. By a result in~\cite{JO.12}, we know that there exists a b-coloring $f$ of $K_p\oblong K_p$ with $2p-3$ colors. We extend this to a b-coloring of $K_q\oblong K_p$ as follows. Denote a vertex of $K_q\oblong K_p$ by $(i,j)$, and let $f'$ be the partial coloring of $K_q\oblong K_p$ initially equal to $f$. For each $i\in \{p+1,\cdots,q\}$, and for each $j\in \{1,\cdots,p\}$ in this order, choose a color for $(i,j)$ not in $f'(V(j)\cup \{(i,1),\cdots,(i,j-1)\})$. Observe that there are at most $i-1+j-1\le p+q-2$
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:457:%In addition, note that $K_p\oblong K_p$ is an induced subgraph of $F=K_{b(G)}\oblong K_{b(H)}$, which by Proposition~\ref{prop:induced} implies that $b(F)\ge 2p-3$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:bspectrum}, we know that $S_b(F)\subseteq S_b(G\oblong H)$; hence $b(G\oblong H)\ge 2p-3$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:458:%Finally, because $b(G)\ge b(H)\ge \chi(H)$, by Theorem~\ref{theo:Cart} we get $b(G\oblong H)\ge b(G)$.%observe that the following equation holds, which finishes the proof by Theorem~\ref{theo:bspecCart}:
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:463:Regarding the direct product, in~\cite{JP.15} the authors observe that $b(G\times H)\ge \max\{b(G),b(H)\}$. Here, we give an alternate proof of this fact and show that when $G$ and $H$ are complete graphs, then there is equality. %Also, we mention that our results also implies $b(G\times H)\ge \{b(G),b(H)\}$, i.e., we provide an alternative proof of this result. 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:466:\begin{theorem}\label{theo:KTV.02}\cite{KTV.02}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:468:\end{theorem}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:470:Observe that the graph in the above theorem is isomorphic to the graph $K_2\times K_n$. In fact, if $G$ is the graph in the theorem above, one can easily verify that the 2-coloring and the $n$-coloring of $G$, which are unique, are also fall-colorings. Therefore, we also have ${\cal F}(G) = \{2,n\}$. This particular fact has been generalized in~\cite{DHHJKLR.00}, where the authors prove that ${\cal F}(K_p\times K_q) = \{p,q\}$. In the next theorem, we generalize both results by proving that in fact $S_b(K_p\times K_q)=\{p,q\}$. We mention that in~\cite{DHHJKLR.00} the authors observe that the theorem below cannot be generalized to the direct product of more than two complete graphs. In particular, they give a fall-coloring with~6 colors of $K_2\times K_3\times K_4$. 
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:472:\begin{theorem}\label{theo:Tensor}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:475:\end{theorem}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:477:Write $V(K_p)$ as $\{u_1,\cdots,u_p\}$ and $V(K_q)$ as $\{v_1,\cdots,v_q\}$. For each $i\in\{1,\cdots,q\}$, denote by $C_i$ the set $V(v_i)$ (the vertices in fiber $v_i$), and for each $i\in \{1,\cdots,p\}$, denote by $R_i$ the set $V(u_i)$ (the vertices in fiber $u_i$). Denote $K_p\times K_q$ by $G$. Note that the coloring $f$ obtained by assigning color $i$ to every vertex in $C_i$, for every $i\in \{1,\cdots, q\}$, is a b-coloring of $G$ with $q$ colors; we say that $f$ is the \emph{column coloring of $G$}. We define the \emph{row coloring of $G$} analogously. Next, we prove that if $f$ is a b-coloring of $G$, then $f$ is either the column coloring or the row coloring of $G$. Because these colorings are also fall-colorings, the theorem follows.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:479:We prove by induction on $q$. If $q=2$, we know it holds by Theorem~\ref{theo:KTV.02}; so suppose that $q\ge 3$. Because $K_p\times K_q$ is  isomorphic to $K_q\times K_q$, we can also suppose that $q\le p$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:482:First, suppose that there exists $i$ such that every vertex in $C_i$ have the same color, say $d$. Let $G_i = G -C_i$, and $f_i$ be equal to $f$ restricted to $G_i$. Note that, because $C_i = f^{-1}(d)$, we get that $f_i$ is a b-coloring of $G_i$, and by induction hypothesis, it is either the row or the column coloring of $G_i$. If $f_i$ is the column coloring, then we are done since it follows that $f$ is the column coloring of $G$. So, $f_i$ must be the row coloring, in which case we can suppose that for each $j\in \{1,\cdots,p\}$ we have that $f((u_j,v_\ell)) = j$ for every $\ell\in \{1,\cdots, q\}\setminus \{i\}$. But observe that, for each $j\in\{1,\cdots,p\}$, we have that $(u_j,v_i)$ misses color $j$, hence $(u_j,v_i)$ cannot be a b-vertex of color $d$. We get a contradiction since $f^{-1}(d) = C_i$.
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:494:Now, without loss of generality, suppose that $R^*=\{1,\cdots, p'\}$ and that $C^*=\{1,\cdots,q'\}$. By~(1), we know that $q'\ge 1$. First, suppose that $p'<p$. By definition, we know that each color appears at most once in $R_i$ for every $i\in \{p'+1,\cdots, p\}$. This means that, for each $j\in C^*$, vertex $(u_1,v_j)$ is not adjacent to color $f((u_{p'+1},v_j))$, a contradiction since in this case, by~(2), color $d_1$ does not have b-vertices. Therefore, we have that $p'=p$. Now, suppose that $q'<q$. By the choice of $q'$, observe that there must exist a color $d\notin \{d_1,\cdots,d_p\}$ such that $f^{-1}(d)\subseteq C_{q'+1}$. Let $D = \{i\in\{1,\cdots,p\}\mid f((u_i,v_{q'+1})) = d\}$ (vertices in fiber $v_{q'+1}$ colored with $d$). Note that for each $i\in D$, we get that vertex $(u_i,v_{q'+1})$ is not adjacent to color $d_i$, a contradiction since in this case color $d$ has no b-vertices. Therefore, we have that $q'=q$, in which case $f$ is the row b-coloring of $G$.
