paper/src/fallbcol.tex:521:\section{Fall coloring and products of general graphs}\label{sec:fallPart}
paper/src/fallbcol.tex:523:We have seen that ${\cal F}(G\odot H)\neq \emptyset$ whenever ${\cal F}(G)\neq \emptyset$ and ${\cal F}(H)\neq \emptyset$, but what happens when one of these sets is empty? Next, we show some situations where we still can obtain a fall-coloring of the product, even though one of the fall-spectra might be empty.
