paper/src/fallbcol.tex:577:%Write $K_p$ as $\{v_1,\cdots,v_p\}$, and let $f$ be an optimal proper coloring of $G\times K_p$. For each $u\in V(G)$, let $V(u) = \{(u,v_1), \cdots, (u,v_p)\}$ and $D_u$ be the set of colors $f(V(u))$. If $\lvert D_u\rvert =p$ for some $u$ then $\chi(G\times K_p) \ge \lvert D_u\vert = p \ge \min\{p,\chi(G)\}$ and we are done. Otherwise, by the pigeonhole principle we know that there is at least one color that appears more than once in $V(u)$, for every $u\in V(G)$. So, for each $u\in V(G)$, let $g(u)$ be such a color. We claim that $g$ is a proper coloring of $V(G)$, in which case we get $\chi(G\times K_p)=\lvert Im(f)\rvert \ge \lvert Im(g)\rvert \ge \chi(G)$ and the theorem follows.
