paper/src/fallbcol.tex:639:%Nevertheless, we mention that we can look at the Hedetniemi's Conjecture in a similar way. For this, define the \emph{coloring spectrum} of a graph $G$ as being the set $C(G)$ containing every $k$ for which $G$ has a proper coloring with $k$ colors; clearly $C(G) = \{k\in\Nat\mid k\ge \chi(G)\}$. The Hedetniemi's Conjecture is then equivalent to saying that $C(G\times H) = C(G)\cup C(H)$. Because $S_b(K_2\times K_3) = {\cal F}(K_2\times K_3)$ and $S_b(K_4) = {\cal F}(K_4)$, the case $K_2\times K_3\times K_4$ in fact tell us that an analogous conjecture does not hold neither for b-colorings nor for fall-colorings. Nevertheless, one may wonder whether at least the following can be ensured.
