/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:463:Regarding the direct product, in~\cite{JP.15} the authors observe that $b(G\times H)\ge \max\{b(G),b(H)\}$. Here, we give an alternate proof of this fact and show that when $G$ and $H$ are complete graphs, then there is equality. %Also, we mention that our results also implies $b(G\times H)\ge \{b(G),b(H)\}$, i.e., we provide an alternative proof of this result. 
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:467:Let $G$ be isomorphic to the complete bipartite graph $K_{n,n}$ minus a perfect matching. Then $S_b(G) = \{2,n\}$.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:470:Observe that the graph in the above theorem is isomorphic to the graph $K_2\times K_n$. In fact, if $G$ is the graph in the theorem above, one can easily verify that the 2-coloring and the $n$-coloring of $G$, which are unique, are also fall-colorings. Therefore, we also have ${\cal F}(G) = \{2,n\}$. This particular fact has been generalized in~\cite{DHHJKLR.00}, where the authors prove that ${\cal F}(K_p\times K_q) = \{p,q\}$. In the next theorem, we generalize both results by proving that in fact $S_b(K_p\times K_q)=\{p,q\}$. We mention that in~\cite{DHHJKLR.00} the authors observe that the theorem below cannot be generalized to the direct product of more than two complete graphs. In particular, they give a fall-coloring with~6 colors of $K_2\times K_3\times K_4$. 
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:477:Write $V(K_p)$ as $\{u_1,\cdots,u_p\}$ and $V(K_q)$ as $\{v_1,\cdots,v_q\}$. For each $i\in\{1,\cdots,q\}$, denote by $C_i$ the set $V(v_i)$ (the vertices in fiber $v_i$), and for each $i\in \{1,\cdots,p\}$, denote by $R_i$ the set $V(u_i)$ (the vertices in fiber $u_i$). Denote $K_p\times K_q$ by $G$. Note that the coloring $f$ obtained by assigning color $i$ to every vertex in $C_i$, for every $i\in \{1,\cdots, q\}$, is a b-coloring of $G$ with $q$ colors; we say that $f$ is the \emph{column coloring of $G$}. We define the \emph{row coloring of $G$} analogously. Next, we prove that if $f$ is a b-coloring of $G$, then $f$ is either the column coloring or the row coloring of $G$. Because these colorings are also fall-colorings, the theorem follows.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:479:We prove by induction on $q$. If $q=2$, we know it holds by Theorem~\ref{theo:KTV.02}; so suppose that $q\ge 3$. Because $K_p\times K_q$ is  isomorphic to $K_q\times K_q$, we can also suppose that $q\le p$.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:482:First, suppose that there exists $i$ such that every vertex in $C_i$ have the same color, say $d$. Let $G_i = G -C_i$, and $f_i$ be equal to $f$ restricted to $G_i$. Note that, because $C_i = f^{-1}(d)$, we get that $f_i$ is a b-coloring of $G_i$, and by induction hypothesis, it is either the row or the column coloring of $G_i$. If $f_i$ is the column coloring, then we are done since it follows that $f$ is the column coloring of $G$. So, $f_i$ must be the row coloring, in which case we can suppose that for each $j\in \{1,\cdots,p\}$ we have that $f((u_j,v_\ell)) = j$ for every $\ell\in \{1,\cdots, q\}\setminus \{i\}$. But observe that, for each $j\in\{1,\cdots,p\}$, we have that $(u_j,v_i)$ misses color $j$, hence $(u_j,v_i)$ cannot be a b-vertex of color $d$. We get a contradiction since $f^{-1}(d) = C_i$.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:494:Now, without loss of generality, suppose that $R^*=\{1,\cdots, p'\}$ and that $C^*=\{1,\cdots,q'\}$. By~(1), we know that $q'\ge 1$. First, suppose that $p'<p$. By definition, we know that each color appears at most once in $R_i$ for every $i\in \{p'+1,\cdots, p\}$. This means that, for each $j\in C^*$, vertex $(u_1,v_j)$ is not adjacent to color $f((u_{p'+1},v_j))$, a contradiction since in this case, by~(2), color $d_1$ does not have b-vertices. Therefore, we have that $p'=p$. Now, suppose that $q'<q$. By the choice of $q'$, observe that there must exist a color $d\notin \{d_1,\cdots,d_p\}$ such that $f^{-1}(d)\subseteq C_{q'+1}$. Let $D = \{i\in\{1,\cdots,p\}\mid f((u_i,v_{q'+1})) = d\}$ (vertices in fiber $v_{q'+1}$ colored with $d$). Note that for each $i\in D$, we get that vertex $(u_i,v_{q'+1})$ is not adjacent to color $d_i$, a contradiction since in this case color $d$ has no b-vertices. Therefore, we have that $q'=q$, in which case $f$ is the row b-coloring of $G$.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:498:%Observe that, since $S_b(G)\neq \emptyset$ for every $G$, we get that the union in Corollary~\ref{cor:bspectrum} is well defined and one can see that, by Theorem~\ref{theo:Tensor}, it equals $S_b(G)\cup S_b(H)$. Thus, we get the following corollary.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:509:By Corollary~\ref{cor:bspectrum}, we know that for every $p\in S_b(G)$ and $q\in S_b(H)$, we have that $S_b(K_p\times K_q)$ is contained in $S_b(G\times H)$. And by Theorem~\ref{theo:Tensor} and the fact that $S_b(F)\neq \emptyset$, for every graph $F$, we get that 
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:512:By Corollary~\ref{cor:FallSpectrum}, the same argument can be applied for fall-colorings as long as the product $K_p\times K_q$ is defined for some value of $p$ and some value of $q$, i.e., as long as ${\cal F}(G)\neq \emptyset$ and ${\cal F}(H)\neq \emptyset$.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:515:Observe that the first part of Theorems~\ref{theo:mainb} and~\ref{theo:mainfall} are given by Corollary~\ref{cor:Lex}, while the cartesian product and the direct product parts are given by Corollaries~\ref{cor:Cart} and~\ref{cor:Direct}, respectively.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:523:We have seen that ${\cal F}(G\odot H)\neq \emptyset$ whenever ${\cal F}(G)\neq \emptyset$ and ${\cal F}(H)\neq \emptyset$, but what happens when one of these sets is empty? Next, we show some situations where we still can obtain a fall-coloring of the product, even though one of the fall-spectra might be empty.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:530:Let $f$ be any fall-coloring of $G$ that uses colors $\{1,\cdots,k\}$, where $k\ge \chi(H)$, and consider an optimal coloring $g$ of $H$ that uses colors $\{1,\cdots,\ell\}$. Then, for each $i\in\{1,\cdots,\ell\}$, let $\pi_i$ denote the permutation \[(i,i+1,\cdots,k,1,\cdots,i-1).\] Note that $\pi_i$ is well defined since $k\ge \ell$. Finally, for each $u\in g^{-1}(i)$,  color the copy of $G$ related to $u$ by using $f$ where the colors are permuted as in $\pi_i$. Let $h$ be the obtained coloring. 
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:538:If $H$ has isolated vertices, so does $G\times H$, and since $\chi(G)\ge 2$, these vertices can never be b-vertices; hence ${\cal F}(G\times H) = \emptyset$. Now, let $f$ be a fall-coloring of $G$ with $k$ colors, and let $g:V(G\times H)\rightarrow \{1,\cdots,k\}$ be defined  as $g((u,v)) = f(u)$, for every $(u,v)\in V(G\times H)$. Consider a color $i$; because $f^{-1}(i)$ is a stable set, as well as  $V(u)$ for every $u\in f^{-1}(i)$, we get that $g^{-1}(i)$ is also a stable set (i.e., $g$ is a proper coloring). Now, consider a vertex $(u,v)\in V(G\times H)$. Since $H$ has no isolated vertices, we get that $v$ must have some neighbor, say $v'$. By definition, we know that $S = \{(u',v')\in V(G\times H)\mid u'\in N(u)\}$ is contained in $N((u,v))$, and because $u$ is a b-vertex in $f$, we know that $f(N(u)) = g(S) = \{1,\cdots,k\}\setminus \{f(u)\}$. It follows that $(u,v)$ is a b-vertex in $g$.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:586:%Clearly $K_{\omega(G)} \Hom G$ and $K_{\omega(H)} \Hom H$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:hom}, we get that $(G\times K_{\omega(H)})\Hom (G\times H)$, and $(K_{\omega(G)}\times H)\Hom (G\times H)$. Now, suppose that $(G\times H) \Hom K_n$. By transitivity, we get $(G\times K_{\omega(H)})\Hom K_n$ and $(K_{\omega(G)}\times H)\Hom K_n$, and by Corollary~\ref{theo:GtimesKp}, we get that $n\ge \min\{\omega(H),\chi(G)\}$ and $n\ge \min\{\omega(G),\chi(H)\}$. The theorem follows since $\chi(G)\ge \omega(G)\ge \omega(H)$. 
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:589:%One can verify that the only situation where the value in the theorem above is smaller than $N=\min\{\chi(G),\chi(H)\}$ is when $\omega(H)\le \omega(G) < N$. This gives us the following. Observe that it implies that the conjecture holds when $G$ or $H$ is a perfect graph.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:608:We have seen that the spectrum of products involving complete graphs play an important role in better understanding the spectrum of general graphs. Then, we set out to investigate the main graph products and have seen that: ${\cal F}(K_p\boxtimes K_q) = S_b(K_p\boxtimes K_q) = \{pq\}$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:Lex});  ${\cal F}(K_p\oblong K_q) = \max\{p,q\}$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:Cart}); and ${\cal F}(K_p\times K_q) = S_b(K_p\times K_q) = \{p,q\}$ (Theorem~\ref{theo:Tensor}). Therefore, the only not completely described set is $S_b(K_p\oblong K_q)$.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:615:We mention that in~\cite{KM.02}, the authors prove that $b(G\oblong H)\ge b(G)+b(H)-1$ under certain conditions. Note that if the answer to the above question is ``yes'', then Corollary~\ref{cor:bspectrum} implies $b(G\oblong H) \ge b(G)+b(H)-3$. This would considerably improve previous results, since the conditions for $b(G\oblong H)\ge b(G)+b(H)-1$ in~\cite{KM.02} are quite strong.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:617:Concerning the existence of fall-colorings, we have seen that ${\cal F}(G\odot H)\neq \emptyset$ whenever ${\cal F}(G)\neq \emptyset$ and ${\cal F}(H)\neq \emptyset$. We also have seen that under some conditions ${\cal F}(G\oblong H)\neq \emptyset$ and ${\cal F}(G\times H)\neq \emptyset$ when ${\cal F}(G)\neq \emptyset$. 
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:618:In~\cite{S.15_2}, the authors observe that the graph $C_5[K_2]$ has a fall-coloring, while we know that $C_5$ has no fall-colorings. Because of the next proposition, we get that $C_5[K_2] \cong C_5\boxtimes K_2$. 
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:621:Let $G$ and $H$ be graphs. Then, $G\boxtimes H\subseteq G[H]$, with equality when $H$ is the complete graph.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:626:Now, suppose that $H$ is the complete graph and let $e=(u_a,v_a)(u_b,v_b)\in E(G[H])$. As before, if the second condition of the definition of the lexicographic product is also one of the conditions in the strong product. So, we can consider that $u_au_b\in E(G)$. In this case, either $v_a=v_b$ in which case $e$ is also in $G\boxtimes H$, or $v_a\neq v_b$ in which case $e$ is in $G\boxtimes K_p$ since $H$ is complete.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:638:%Observe that another way of looking at the questions above is the following: given graphs $G$ and $H$ and a fall-coloring $f$ of $G\odot H$, is it true that $f$ must define a fall-coloring on either $G$ or $H$? In Section~\ref{sec:products}, we have seen that this is not the case, since $6\in {\cal F}(K_2\times K_3\times K_4)$. 
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:639:%Nevertheless, we mention that we can look at the Hedetniemi's Conjecture in a similar way. For this, define the \emph{coloring spectrum} of a graph $G$ as being the set $C(G)$ containing every $k$ for which $G$ has a proper coloring with $k$ colors; clearly $C(G) = \{k\in\Nat\mid k\ge \chi(G)\}$. The Hedetniemi's Conjecture is then equivalent to saying that $C(G\times H) = C(G)\cup C(H)$. Because $S_b(K_2\times K_3) = {\cal F}(K_2\times K_3)$ and $S_b(K_4) = {\cal F}(K_4)$, the case $K_2\times K_3\times K_4$ in fact tell us that an analogous conjecture does not hold neither for b-colorings nor for fall-colorings. Nevertheless, one may wonder whether at least the following can be ensured.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:645:%Observe that the analogous question for the b-spectrum is equivalent to proving the Hedetniemi's Conjecture since there always exists a b-coloring of $G\times H$ with $\chi(G\times H)$ colors.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:651:Does the following hold whenever $G$ is a perfect graph?
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:661:Nevertheless, one can still ask about the maximal subclasses of the perfect graphs for which the answer is ``yes''. For instance, it has been proved to hold for threshold graphs and split graphs~\cite{KM.16}, and for strongly chordal graphs~\cite{LDL.05} In particular, we pose the following question.
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:664:Can one decide in polynomial time whether a chordal graph $G$ is such that ${\cal F}(G)\neq \emptyset$?
/tmp/1607.08453-srcdir/fallbcol.tex:691:%Let $f$ be an epimorphism from $H$ to $F$. Denote $G\odot H, G\odot F$ by $H',F'$, respectively, and let $g:V(H')\rightarrow V(F')$ define as $g((u,v)) = (u,f(v))$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:hom}, we know that $g$ is a homomorphism. So, consider an edge $\alpha\beta\in E(F')$; it remains to prove that there exists $\alpha'\beta'\in E(H')$ such that $g(\alpha')=\alpha$ and $g(\beta')=\beta$. Write $\alpha$ as $(u_a,v_a)$ and $\beta$ as $(u_b,v_b)$. If $v_av_b\in E(F)$, then let $v'_av'_b\in E(G)$ be such that $f(v'_a)=v_a$ and $f(v'_b)=v_b$. Also, let $\alpha'=(u_a,v'_a)$ and $\beta'=(u_b,v'_b)$. Clearly, the basic formulas related to $(u_a,v_a)$ and $(u_b,v_b)$ have the same values as the related basic formulas of $(u_a,v'_a)$ and $(u_b,v'_b)$; hence $\alpha'\beta'\in E(G')$ and, since $g(\alpha') = \alpha$ and $g(\beta')=\beta$, we have the desired pair. Now, if $v_a=v_b$, then let $v'_a\in f^{-1}(v_a)$ (it exists since $f$ is surjective), and let $\alpha'=(u_a,v'_a)$ and $\beta'=(u_b,v'_a)$. Again, one can verify that the basic formulas related to $(u_a,v_a)$ and $(u_b,v_a)$ have the same values as the ones related to $(u_a,v'_a)$ and $(u_b,v'_a)$. Finally, if $v_a\neq v_b$ and $v_av_b\notin E(F)$, then let $v'_a\in f^{-1}(v_a)$ and $v'_b\in f^{-1}(v_b)$. Since $f$ is an homomorphism, we get that $v'_av'_b\notin E(G)$ and, again, the basic formulas of $(u_a,v_a),(u_b,v_b)$ and of $(u_a,v'_a),(u_b,v'_b)$ have same value. 

